@352Nole Read above. This is a pro sport now with paid professional athletes. Time to draw the line. Let the G6 have their own championship. Let the independents join a conference or play in the G6 which is what ND does any way. 

In a professional sport now. If you want to play for it all, be one of the two best teams in your conference. You don't think in pro ball ... which is what CFB is now ... there are better teams that don't make it because they're in the same division? 

As for who's favorite on a neutral field, I reject the gambling concept of team strength and using that for rankings. Time to reign this in and do it the right way.

@TheIndiefiant Of course we can. The goal, as it should be every year, is to win your conference. It doesn't matter how mnay teams a conference has because the cream will rise to the top. Also, every team doesn't have to play each other through a season. While we can take some cues from the NFL, we can't duplicate this in a realistic way. 

What we can do however, is deal with what we got and understand that the playoff isn't the goal. The goal is to win your conference. The goal is to make the regular season mean something again. The players will need contracts that can't be stymied by a local judge or some money grubbing lawyer just like the NFL has. CFB needs rules that can't be questioned or litigated. 

CFB is a professional league now, filled with professional players with no rules except you need to go to that school. Yet here we are trying to make sense of a system that no longer exists. This new professional league needs to crown a champion based on what happens on the field not a beauty contest where people who don't even play the game decide who gets in and who gets left out. This needs to stop. I'm just offering one way to do this while keeping the real gen of CFB ... the regular season ... intact.

@MattyO-72 Oklahoma is fairly ugly however a rivalry game so those things happen, see tOSU in '24 I still for the life of me don't understand how they lost.  Florida shouldn't be ranked but still a 30-35 type team.  

I do think they overcorrected from previous seasons where they tended to just look at records a little bit much, and they still kind of did that 1-4.  Miami has one fairly nice win,  Utah the same.  UGA played you and me in the non conference, same with Indiana.  UCLA and ND don't really have a win to hang on to.  

If a 4th loss hits Texas they'll also be out so it's moot, even a 3rd one would be costly.  There won't likely be 10 teams that finish with  2 or less anyway given all the games these teams face each other, many of them multiple times not to mention losses that won't make any sense.

@brob7427By giving Bama 5 and UCLA 8, you are literally devaluing winning and saying losing games is better if its against slightly better opponents. This would make sense if both teams were undefeated. But the fact of the matter is that Bama has lost twice; UCLA hasn't lost at all.

When should we ever be celebrating losing to stronger opponents over winning all your games? You have to play who is in front of you, why are we punishing UCLA for doing that and BEATING everyone, when Bama couldn't accomplish the same thing? 

It just doesnt add up without introducing some kind of unfair bias into the conversation.

@senpaiofsavage3072 their post season performance is making it to the National Championship game, Einstein. You’re talking about decades ago? College football teams are different year to year, even more so coach to coach, and even more so since NIL. Welcome to 2026. 

Furthermore, Notre Dame didn’t avoid a conference; the conferences avoided them. In particular, Notre Dame lobbied to enter the Big10 for years, yet Michigan ensured they never got in. Eventually the gave up and decided staying independent made more sense financially, as it does. It’s a huge blessing and they’re going to be one of the most dominant teams for decades because of it. Remember that whole NIL thing? I know the world is a lot different now and hard to understand, yet Notre Dame has a LOT of money. Get use to it. 

Lastly, the only series Notre Dame ever had to cancel was Michigan which their agreement with the ACC to play 6 games per season required a series to drop. I would love for that game to come back, yet it would require dropping another rivalry game, so it’s a tough spot. Either way, your representation of them canceling games because they’re “scared” is pathetic and sad. You don’t know much about anything to do with this.

